Q: Codeigniter pagination links only increment the offset by 1 per page A: <p>Controllers should not directly interact with the database, so I moved the total count to the model and consolidated the two required queries into one method.</p>
<p>Controller</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function index(int $offset = 0)
{
    $config = [
        'base_url' =&gt; base_url('products/index'),
        'uri_segment' =&gt; 3,
        'num_links' =&gt; 10,
        'cur_page' =&gt; $offset,
        'per_page' =&gt; 10, // this is the library default
    ];

    [$config['total_rows'], $data['products']] = $this-&gt;products_model-&gt;getPaginatedProducts($config['per_page'], $offset);

    $this-&gt;load-&gt;library('pagination');
    $this-&gt;pagination-&gt;initialize($config);

    $this-&gt;load-&gt;view('products', $data);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Model</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function getPaginatedProducts(?int $limit, ?int $offset): array
{
    return [
        $this-&gt;db-&gt;count_all_results('product', false),
        $this-&gt;db-&gt;get('', $limit, $offset)-&gt;result(),
    ];
}
</code></pre>
<p><sup>As seen <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79761194/2943403">here</a></sup></p>
<p>View</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php
var_export($products);

echo $this-&gt;pagination-&gt;create_links();
?&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>See the library class: <a href="https://github.com/bcit-ci/CodeIgniter/blob/develop/system/libraries/Pagination.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/bcit-ci/CodeIgniter/blob/develop/system/libraries/Pagination.php</a></p>

Q: Iterating a 2D Array Diagonally in Python A: <p>Use a generator function. By adjusting the inner loop range, the diagonal coordinate (k, k) is only printed once.</p>
<pre><code>import numpy as np

mat = np.zeros(shape=(10, 10), dtype='&lt;U2')
for i in range(10):
    for j in range(10):
        mat[i][j] = f&quot;{i}{j}&quot;

def shell_traversal(matrix):
    max_dim = max(matrix.shape)
    for k in range(max_dim):
        # Yield the corner element (k, k) exactly once
        yield (k, k), matrix[k, k]

        # Yield the rest of the row and column shell up to k
        for i in range(k):
            yield (i, k), matrix[i, k]
            yield (k, i), matrix[k, i]

# Example usage with your early stopping threshold
THRESHOLD = &quot;23&quot; 
for (i, j), value in shell_traversal(mat):
    print((i, j), value)
    if value == THRESHOLD:
        print(&quot;Threshold reached. Stopping.&quot;)
        break
</code></pre>
<p>Or, if you want the exact mathematical order where items are grouped by their distance from the origin (i + j = k), you can iterate by the sum of indices. This completely avoids checking any element twice.</p>
<pre><code>def anti_diagonal_traversal(matrix):
    rows, cols = matrix.shape
    # The maximum possible sum of indices is (rows - 1) + (cols - 1)
    for k in range(rows + cols - 1):
        for i in range(max(0, k - cols + 1), min(rows, k + 1)):
            j = k - i
            yield (i, j), matrix[i, j]
</code></pre>
